# Which benchmarks are meant to exist, as `package<TAB>name`.
#
# scripts/run_benchmarks.sh compares this file against everything it can find
# in the tree and fails until the two agree. The comparison is what makes a
# DELETED benchmark loud: an enumeration checked only against itself would
# simply produce a smaller set, match everything in it, and report success.
#
# It is also what makes a benchmark this platform cannot compile visible at
# all. `go test -list` answers only for the configuration it is running in, so
# a `//go:build windows` benchmark is invisible to it on a Linux runner. Every
# tracked test file is therefore also parsed with go/parser, which has no build
# configuration to be blind to, and anything it finds that the toolchain cannot
# build is listed here too — alongside an entry in BENCHMARKS_NOT_RUN_IN_CI
# saying it cannot run.
#
# Adding or removing a benchmark fails CI until this file is updated in the
# same change. That is the point — the diff here is the only place anyone
# states that a benchmark is meant to be gone rather than accidentally
# missing.
#
# To update: run scripts/run_benchmarks.sh and apply the diff it prints. There
# is no regeneration command, deliberately: one would have to run under every
# build configuration to be correct, and a command that is right on Linux and
# quietly wrong on Windows is worse than a diff a human applies.
github.com/shardpilot/shardpilot-go	BenchmarkBuildBatchMapPayloads
github.com/shardpilot/shardpilot-go	BenchmarkCompressBatchBody
github.com/shardpilot/shardpilot-go	BenchmarkEnqueueMapPayload
github.com/shardpilot/shardpilot-go	BenchmarkStatsCollectorAggregation
github.com/shardpilot/shardpilot-go/pkg/crash	BenchmarkClientEmit
